  "strategy": "[1] Approach and Rationale\nWe propose a hierarchical optimization approach combining convex relaxations with pattern evolutionary search, specifically designed to escape local optima in the non-convex Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem. Unlike previous methods focusing on sequential optimization phases, this strategy employs:\n\n1. Multi-resolution hierarchical structure: Represent solutions at multiple scales (400, 600, 900 points) with smooth interpolation preserving integral constraints\n2. Convex-relaxed subproblems: Use DCP-compliant surrogate objectives to find provable lower bounds\n3. Pattern-based evolution: Perform cross-resolution pattern discovery using projected gradient methods\n4. Adaptive search budget: Dynamically allocate computation between global exploration and local refinement\n\nThe approach targets the optimization difficulties: (1) variable dominance in linear correlation constraints (2) lack of differentiable pathways for high-resolution transitions (3) premature convergence in local searches.\n\n[2] Mathematical Specification\nWe represent solutions via piecewise linear interpolation between anchor points:\nh(x) = \u03a3\u1d62 h\u1d62 * B\u1d62(x) where B\u1d62 are basis functions allowing:\n- Explicit enforcement of 0\u2264h\u22641\n- Integral preservation via dual-sum constraint\n- Cross-resolution mapping via finite-element projection\n\nConvex relaxations consider:\nMin z\nsubject to:\n\u2211h\u1d62 = n/2  (integral constraint)\n0 \u2264 h\u1d62 \u2264 1  (box constraints)\n1\u1d40h \u2264 z  (with correlation matrix W)\n\nPattern transitions are governed by linear transport operators:\n\nT(h) = argmin_{\u2016q\u2016=1} (h\u207b - T(h))\u1d40W(h\u207a-T(h)) + \u03b3L(q)\n\nWhere W captures the correlation structure, L(q) is L2 regularization, and \u03b3 controls emphasis.\n\n[3] Implementation Procedure\n1. Initialization:\n- Generate population of size 20 with:\n  - Clamped random samples projected to feasible space\n  - Multi-resolution interpolations of parent solution\n  - Strategic random walks with gradient guidance ($P=\u2192\u2207C\u2075$)\n\n2. Evolutionary phase:\n- Pattern identification: For each resolution $n\u2208{400,600,900}$\n  - Compute projected gradient flows\n  - Identify dominant motifs using LDA projection\n  - Cross-fertilize successful patterns between resolutions\n- Population updates:\n  - Mutation: Adaptive DE with dimension-wise mutation\n  - Crossover: Exponential blending with constraint preservation\n  - Selection: Fitness-proportion selection with hierarchical comparison\n\n3. Local refinement:\n- For elite candidates (top 5% across scales):\n  - Perform projected gradient descent\n  - Use dual-ascent for bound tightening\n  - Apply stochastic rounding to enhance feasibility\n\n4. Adaptation rules:\n- Resolution change: When local score trend stabilizes and decreases, \n  - Increase to next scale \u00d71.5 with interpolated initialization\n- Budget allocation: \n  - Evolution: 60% (global search)\n  - Refinement: 40% (local search)\n  - Time checkpoints at 30%, 70%, 90% of total budget\n\n5. Feasibility checks:\n- Maintain integral constraint via $proj_{sum=\u00bdn}$ operation\n- Clip [0,1] bounds with logarithmic stretching to improve condition number\n- Verify score re-matches at each resolution level\n\n[4] Search Choices and Variations\n\nDefault configuration:\n- Population size: 20\n- Genetic algorithm parameters:\n  - Crossover rate: 0.8\n  - Mutation rate: 0.25\n- Gradient descent parameters:\n  - Line search type: Wolfe\n  - Maximum iterations: 1000\n  - Step tolerance: 1e-6\n- Resolution jumps: \n  - From 400 to 600 at score improvement threshold 0.002\n  - From 600 to 900 at score improvement threshold 0.001\n\nConsequential variations:\n1. Heuristic Trigger Variations:\n   a) Pattern shift detection: Trigger when motif entropy increases malformedly (trigger temp=0.7)\n   b) Flatness detection: Trigger when gradient norm falls below adaptive threshold (trigger temp=0.1)\n2. Representation Variations:\n   a) Directed transport: Use Poisson process embedding (increases budget)\n   b) Adaptive grid: Dynamic clustering of grid points (2x computation)\n3. Optimization Variations:\n   a) Weighted L1: Switch L2 to L1 regularization for sparsity\n   b) Dual decomposition: Add auxiliary variable master problem (complexity O(n\u00b3))\n\n[5] Compute Budget and Robustness\n\nEstimates:\n- Low-resolution evaluation: ~2.5 ms\n- High-resolution evaluation: ~15 ms\n- Evolutionary trial: ~20 ms (including constraints)\n- Gradient descent: ~50 ms (worst-case)\n\nFeasible schedule:\n1. Initial population: \n   - Build with automated interpolation: 0.3s\n   - Score evaluation: 4 parallel (\u00d72) / 2 = 1.6s\n\n2. Evolution phases:\n- Global evolution checkpoints:\n  - Score reevaluation: time = 0.4*(budget_s-2)\n  - Pattern extraction: time = 0.2*(budget_s-1)\n  - Local refinement: time = 0.1*(budget_s-3)\n\nTermination conditions:\n- Memory: Maintain population size 20 at each active resolution\n- Time: \n  - Safety margin: 15% within each phase\n  - Early termination: if score stabilizes +\u03b5 for 2 consecutive checkpoints\n\nFallback procedure:\n- Retain rank-ordered elites by score\n- Return parent solution if requested resolution exceeds 900\n- Reconstruct interpolations from stored checkpoints\n\n[6] Validation and Return Contract\n\nCandidate validation:\n1. Integral check: sum(h) must equal 0.5*n (within 1e-6)\n2. Bounds check: each h(i) \u2208 [0,1]\n3. Score verification: recompute correlation using double precision\n4. Motif check: detect if solution represents novel pattern structure\n\nScore consistency checks:\n- Multi-resolution consistency: |C5(n) - C5(m)| <= \u03b5||dh||/min(n,m)\n- Evaluate benchmark: compare against alternating phase solution\n\nFinite difference verification:\n- Conduct gradient check for top 10% candidates\n- Implement tolerance: 1e-4 relative error\n- For stochastic variants, use directional derivative \n\nReturn format:\nnp.array(h) with length n_points, where n_points \u2208 {400,600,900} maintained by user choice or adaptive optimization\nc5_bound = computed correlation maximum \u00d7 dx\nn_points = dimension of h\n\nRecovery mechanism:\n- Failed constraints: project \u2192 feasible solution using minimal perturbation\n- Invalid scores: fall back to alternating-phase reconstruction\n- Evaluator mismatch: compute both circulant and linear correlation variants\n\nFull validation re-run:\n- Verify integral is exactly n/2 (given dx)\n- Confirm 0\u2264h\u22641 at all points\n- Check score with both:\n  - mode=\"full\" correlation (as in parent)\n  - circulant correlation adjustment (for aspect ratio)\n\nFinite sanity checks:\n- Initialize with parent solution\n- Run both phases on sample candidate\n- Check score consistency between phases",
